# The board's whole point is that its state is machine-local and transient: it
# describes agents alive on this machine right now. Committing any of it would
# publish one machine's live claims and conflict on every pull.
sessions/
messages/
read/
paths/
locks/
wins/

# Written by every install as the pre-edit undo. Once history is tracked, git is
# the durable one; this stays local for the edit that is not committed yet.
bin/agentboard.prev

# Which set of names this machine's agents are given. A preference belonging to
# whoever is sitting here, not to the repo.
/theme

# Theme packs written on this machine. They override what the repo ships, which is
# only useful if a pull never argues with them.
/themes.local/

# Which repos on this machine are worked in which way. Describes a workspace, not
# this tool — and naming a team's repos is exactly what should not ship.
/workspace.local
